In .NET MAUI, theme detection and control is built into the `Application` class.

## Detect Current Theme

```csharp
AppTheme currentTheme = Application.Current.RequestedTheme;
// Returns AppTheme.Light, AppTheme.Dark, or AppTheme.Unspecified
```

## Subscribe to Theme Changes

```csharp
Application.Current.RequestedThemeChanged += (s, e) =>
{
    AppTheme newTheme = e.RequestedTheme;
    Debug.WriteLine($"Theme changed to: {newTheme}");
    // Update UI or trigger re-theming logic here
};
```

This fires in real time when the user toggles dark mode in device settings.

## Force a Specific Theme

```csharp
// Override system theme
Application.Current.UserAppTheme = AppTheme.Dark;

// Revert to following the system setting
Application.Current.UserAppTheme = AppTheme.Unspecified;
```

## Respond in XAML with `AppThemeBinding`

```xml
<Label Text="Hello"
       TextColor="{AppThemeBinding Light=Black, Dark=White}"
       BackgroundColor="{AppThemeBinding Light=White, Dark=#1E1E1E}" />
```

These bindings update automatically when the theme changes — no manual refresh needed.

## Typical Wiring in `App.xaml.cs`

```csharp
public partial class App : Application
{
    public App()
    {
        InitializeComponent();
        MainPage = new AppShell();

        RequestedThemeChanged += OnThemeChanged;
    }

    private void OnThemeChanged(object sender, AppThemeChangedEventArgs e)
    {
        Debug.WriteLine($"System theme is now: {e.RequestedTheme}");
        // e.g. swap merged resource dictionaries, update preferences, etc.
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- `RequestedTheme` → read-only, reflects OS setting.
- `UserAppTheme` → read/write, overrides OS setting; set to `Unspecified` to follow the system again.
- `AppThemeBinding` in XAML reacts to both system and user-forced changes automatically.
